WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.000
Pixar’s critically acclaimed blockbuster "Cars"&nbsp;
came out nearly two decades ago, so a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.520
kids who were mesmerized by the movie in 2006 are&nbsp;
now old enough to notice its many other layers —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.800
including jokes, references and, quite frankly,&nbsp;
some very weird things that only adults noticed.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.720
Adult humor
 
There aren't any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.440
outright dirty jokes hidden in "Cars," but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.600
are a lot of suggestive ones that sail right&nbsp;
over the heads of its five-year-old fans.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.040
For example, Lightning McQueen is a famous race&nbsp;
car, and he's got the groupies to prove it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.360
After his big race at the beginning of&nbsp;
the movie, two red cars named Mia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.960
Tia proclaim to be his "biggest fans" and then&nbsp;
literally flash him with their headlights. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.720
in the movie's central location of&nbsp;
the sleepy town of Radiator Springs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.200
Lightning meets Sally, who has a&nbsp;
"tribal" style "pin-striping tattoo"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.160
just above her bumper — the "Cars" equivalent&nbsp;
of a "tramp stamp" lower-back tattoo.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.080
In another scene, Lightning barges in on Doc&nbsp;
Hudson in his garage — or rather doctor's office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.920
where he's got Sheriff elevated&nbsp;
to examine his undercarriage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.120
Sheriff asks Lightning if he&nbsp;
got, quote, "a good peek."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.520
When Lightning tells Mater and the other&nbsp;
cars that he discovered Doc Hudson was once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.720
a three-time winner of the illustrious racing&nbsp;
championship the Piston Cup, Mater shouts out:

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.360
“He did what in his cup?”

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.560
If you don’t get it, say "Piston&nbsp;
Cup" out loud three times fast.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.800
Name references
 
The names of characters — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.600
the actors filmmakers hired to play those&nbsp;
characters — offer many references that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.000
parents in the know, old movie fans,&nbsp;
and car enthusiasts would be hip to.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.920
For example, the late comedian and countercultural&nbsp;
icon George Carlin gave voice to Fillmore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.560
a hippie Volkswagen bus — the kind&nbsp;
of car countless real hippies drove.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.720
Champion race car "The King" is voiced by Richard&nbsp;
Petty, the NASCAR legend known as "The King."

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.000
Even Ray Magliozzi and his late brother&nbsp;
Tom, a.k.a. Click and Clack, longtime hosts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.360
of National Public Radio mainstay "Car Talk,"&nbsp;
have cameos. They play the owners of Rust-eze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.760
the "bumper ointment" company that sponsors&nbsp;
Lightning McQueen. Radiator Springs' diner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.680
equivalent, Flo's V8 Cafe, is operated by a&nbsp;
former show car named Flo. Her job on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.920
primarily consists of server-like duties. This&nbsp;
parallels that of Florence Jean Castleberry, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.200
just Flo, the sassy waitress, played by Polly&nbsp;
Holliday, on the popular '70s sitcom "Alice."

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.080
Brutally violent races

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.960
As in the human world, car racing is a&nbsp;
hugely popular sport in "Cars." But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.560
cars aren't driving automobiles,&nbsp;
like humans do in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.880
Instead, they're essentially racing&nbsp;
around a track using their actual bodies.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.320
That means auto racing in "Cars" is essentially&nbsp;
a super-fast, super-competitive track and field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.680
event. It's also very high-stakes and highly&nbsp;
competitive. Cars bump each other and jockey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.160
for position at high speeds, leaving each&nbsp;
other in the dust or slammed against the wall.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.440
Spinouts, collisions, and accidents happen&nbsp;
relatively frequently in real-world auto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.440
racing events. But the cars are so&nbsp;
packed with protective equipment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.720
that drivers rarely get hurt. The&nbsp;
vehicles take a lot of damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.720
but fans don't think too much about&nbsp;
it because those cars don't feel pain.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.760
But they do in "Cars." Think about it:&nbsp;
Even the most minor racing incident is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.800
more or less, a horrific injury. Getting a&nbsp;
piece of the car's body knocked off? That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:25.280
like if a human runner lost a body part, mid-race.&nbsp;
A blown tire? The equivalent of a shattered foot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.000
Viewed this way, the aggressive racing style&nbsp;
of the villainous Chick Hicks — which causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.680
the King to roll over multiple times — equates to&nbsp;
gladiatorial combat with intent to harm or kill.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:36.560
The Pizza Planet truck

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.520
Pixar is known for lacing&nbsp;
its films with well-hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.640
jokes and Easter eggs, and "Cars" is no exception.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.920
Ever since its first appearance in "Toy Story,"&nbsp;
the beat-up yellow Pizza Planet delivery truck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.880
has made an appearance in almost every Pixar&nbsp;
movie. So, it makes perfect sense that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.080
show up in "Cars," a movie populated entirely&nbsp;
with motorized vehicles. The Pizza Planet truck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.880
appears for just a quick flash as part of a crowd&nbsp;
scene before one of Lightning McQueen's big races.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.680
Lightning McQueen’s travel arrangements

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.840
In creating the "Cars" world, everything&nbsp;
about the human world had to be translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.280
into its car equivalent. Usually, it works: "Cow&nbsp;
tipping" has been replaced with "tractor tipping,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.520
and Flo's V8 Café serves gas and&nbsp;
oil rather than food, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.000
However, not everything is a direct parallel,&nbsp;
making for some very strange moments.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.480
One example of this is how Lightning&nbsp;
McQueen travels from race to race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.160
As is the case with a real-world race&nbsp;
car, he's hauled around in the back of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.520
a specially-built truck. In "Cars," that truck&nbsp;
is presented as something between a private jet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.920
and a limousine, stocked with amenities&nbsp;
to ensure maximum comfort for Lightning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.760
But because this is the world of sentient&nbsp;
cars, the truck is a living thing.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.680
This means Mack drives down the&nbsp;
freeway with Lightning McQueen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.360
literally inside of him. This is akin to a private&nbsp;
jet pilot who keeps rich clients in his stomach.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.320
Where does 'Cars' take place?

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.000
Kids can watch "Cars" on a surface level: Cars&nbsp;
do stuff, cars learn lessons, cars race. Older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.800
people, however, may be left wondering: How&nbsp;
and why is the world of "Cars" this way?

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.440
It's about a place virtually identical to 21st&nbsp;
century Earth, only everything is built for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:15.200
cars rather than humans. But they also enjoy human&nbsp;
culture that seems based on our real-world things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.520
celebrities and pop culture moments. Radiator&nbsp;
Springs is situated on the disused Route 66.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:27.680
So what's going on here, exactly? Does "Cars" take&nbsp;
place in an alternate dimension where automobiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.320
are the dominant species and humans don't&nbsp;
exist? Or is this some sort of post-apocalyptic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.000
post-human Earth where cars have evolved and taken&nbsp;
over for the now-extinct human race? Either option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.880
feels like it could just as easily be the plot of&nbsp;
a horror movie, rather than a film for children.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.800
“What? This place is crazy!”

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:45.680
Product placement

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.480
Younger viewers in Pixar's target audience&nbsp;
for the film may not even be able to read yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.320
but they probably still wouldn't understand&nbsp;
the adult nature of the fictional products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.960
being pitched in "Cars." For example, Mack&nbsp;
and Lightning McQueen drive past the "Top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.200
Down Truckstop," which proudly boasts its&nbsp;
"convertible waitresses." In the land of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.320
"Cars," it means that the servers can take their&nbsp;
tops down. In other words, they're topless.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.200
Lightning McQueen's primary race sponsor&nbsp;
is Rust-eze, a "bumper ointment."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.760
It's a topical cream for a car's rear-end,&nbsp;
or the automotive equivalent of Preparation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.800
H, a product humans use to treat hemorrhoids.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.920
A major supplier of gasoline to the cars of "Cars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.280
is Dinoco. As gas is food to automobiles,&nbsp;
this would make it the equivalent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.240
a human food conglomerate. Adult viewers&nbsp;
may also recognize it as a link to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.680
Pixar movies — the Pizza Planet truck in "Toy&nbsp;
Story" stops in to refuel at a Dinoco station.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:40.640
Auto-specific names

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.160
While some of the car characters in&nbsp;
"Cars" perfectly embody their name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.880
others are known by names that reflect their&nbsp;
make, model, or function — designations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:54.160
would be clear only to adults, or viewers with at&nbsp;
least a passing knowledge of automobile history.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.800
For instance, in his career as a race car,&nbsp;
Lightning McQueen gets around from race to race in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.240
the back of a big Mack truck — a friendly, helpful&nbsp;
behemoth whose name is literally Mack. Prickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.280
Radiator Springs physician Doc Hudson is named&nbsp;
after, and has the appearance of, a Hudson Hornet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:16.000
a super-fast car of 1950s vintage. Differently,&nbsp;
local hotel operator Sally Carrera gets her name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.840
from the Carrera model of Porsche, of which she is&nbsp;
an example. Radiator Springs town founder Stanley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.400
is named for the Stanley Steamer, an early,&nbsp;
steam-powered car, while his surviving spouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.920
Lizzie, gets her name from the nickname for the&nbsp;
Ford Model T — also known as a "Tin Lizzie."

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:32.640
Old Hollywood rivals

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.720
Radiator Springs' old town doctor Doc&nbsp;
Hudson is voiced by late Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.320
legend Paul Newman, who also raced professionally.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.800
Older viewers of "Cars" may remember that&nbsp;
Newman played a speedy driver on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.120
in the 1969 movie "Winning," which&nbsp;
hit theaters a year after "Bullitt,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.840
an action-packed police movie that featured&nbsp;
one of the most legendary car chase scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:58.960
ever shot. That film starred Steve McQueen,&nbsp;
who famously did much of his own stunt work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.600
and had earned a reputation in films like&nbsp;
"The Great Escape" as a fearless driver.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.760
McQueen was a hotshot on the&nbsp;
asphalt, on-screen and off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.040
not unlike Lightning McQueen,&nbsp;
with whom the actor shares a name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.080
So, in the first "Cars" movie, Doc Hudson is&nbsp;
initially dismissive and openly hostile toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.520
Lightning — with the animosity alluding to a&nbsp;
real-life conflict between McQueen and Newman.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.920
At the peak of their success in the&nbsp;
1960s and 1970s, Paul Newman and Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:30.160
McQueen were seen as Hollywood rivals,&nbsp;
often competing for the same roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.400
McQueen was nearly cast in "Butch Cassidy&nbsp;
and the Sundance Kid," but turned the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.040
down when he learned Newman would be getting&nbsp;
top billing. McQueen later made a passion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.960
project out of the 1971 film "Le Mans," where&nbsp;
he played a driver who delivered the famed line:

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:55.440
"Racing is life. Anything that happens&nbsp;
before or after is just waiting."

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:01.440
Paul Newman took things one step further in 1979,&nbsp;
when he raced a Porsche 935 in the real Le Mans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.680
finishing first in his class and&nbsp;
second overall in a rain-soaked race.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.400
In 1974, the duo were paired together&nbsp;
onscreen for the first and only time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.640
in the blockbuster film "The Towering&nbsp;
Inferno." When McQueen got his script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.920
for the Irwin Allen disaster flick, he counted&nbsp;
out his lines to see if he had more than Newman.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:20.560
Remaking 'Doc Hollywood'

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.680
While on the surface it's a kids' movie about&nbsp;
talking cars, Pixar's "Cars" is a poignant tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:31.280
of redemption, of one individual realizing what's&nbsp;
important in life. This is a story of choices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:36.720
regrets, and one laced with melancholy, which has&nbsp;
way more appeal to adults than it does to kids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.760
It also might feel familiar, because older viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.840
may already have consumed this&nbsp;
story in a very similar form.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.880
In 1991, Michael J. Fox starred in the&nbsp;
live-action comedy movie "Doc Hollywood."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.840
A moderate box office hit that lived on for years&nbsp;
thanks to home video and cable TV re-airings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.840
it has almost the exact same plot&nbsp;
as "Cars," released 15 years later.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.440
Fox played cocky young plastic surgeon Dr.&nbsp;
Ben Stone, driving across the country in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:08.640
red sports car until he crashes it in Grady,&nbsp;
South Carolina. As a sentence for the property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.200
damage he causes, he's forced to work in the&nbsp;
local hospital for a while. He decides to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.840
in Grady after falling in love with charming&nbsp;
ambulance driver Lou, played by Julie Warner.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.480
In "Cars," cocky young car Lightning&nbsp;
McQueen is riding across the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.640
on his way to a race until he&nbsp;
crashes in Radiator Springs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.880
and as a sentence for the property damage&nbsp;
he causes, he's forced to repair the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.760
He decides to stay in Radiator Springs after&nbsp;
falling for charming motel proprietor Sally.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:36.480
Richard Petty references

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.840
The most idolized race car in "Cars" is&nbsp;
easily The King. His name is, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.160
The King, and that nickname comes straight&nbsp;
from Richard "The King" Petty, the all-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.320
winningest racer in NASCAR history&nbsp;
who voices his Pixar counterpart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.240
Older viewers and racing fans may notice&nbsp;
a couple of famous on-the-track events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.080
from Petty's illustrious career&nbsp;
adapted into the action of "Cars."

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.760
In the film's climactic race, The King suffers&nbsp;
a horrific spin-out and crash, mirroring one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:09.760
the real Petty endured in the 1988 Daytona 500.&nbsp;
In a show of remarkable sportsmanship and growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.800
for the once incredibly selfish individual,&nbsp;
Lightning McQueen cuts short his assured win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:20.720
to push The King across the finish line. A&nbsp;
similar event went down at Daytona in 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.960
although it was Petty's pit crew that helped&nbsp;
him finish the race, not another racer.

